  "reasoning": "The claim that humans only use 10% of their brain capacity is a widely debunked neuroscientific myth. Brain imaging studies, such as functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) and positron emission tomography (PET), demonstrate that virtually all regions of the brain are active and serve dedicated functions throughout the day, even during sleep. If 90% of the brain were truly dormant, damage to those areas would cause no noticeable deficit, whereas in reality, damage to almost any area of the brain results in measurable functional or cognitive loss.",
